Built with the installed afkv2 beta (dogfood run, #153). Owner's request, 2026-11-03.

**Pain.** Pages written by afkv2 skills (grill, propose-solution, to-subtasks) are harder to follow than the hand-made v1 review page the owner keeps coming back to (`afk-v2-scope.html`, scope map row `#s-w1`). `visualize` says how to write each component, but nothing says how to organize a page and its content so the presentation is intuitive.

**Want.** A `lavish` skill that governs how an agent writes a page:
- how to organize the page and write its content for an intuitive presentation;
- how to answer the human's notes (see below);
- it uses `visualize` internally for the visual presentations.

**Reference.** The owner's page `afk-v2-scope.html`, and `page-study.md`, a study of what makes it work and what tires the reader. Both are kept outside the repository, in `afk-driver-worktrees/_reference/163/` on the owner's machine: the page holds the owner's notes and work context, and this repository is public. Read them there; never commit them.

**When we pick it up.** Go through the reference page first, the scope map row `#s-w1` and card R ("Your notes and my answers") above all. The session that wrote it had no skill, and it still answered every owner note well. From that page, the agents formulate the instructions, so that future grill sessions reach the same quality. Note answering in particular, as card R does it:
- one row per note, or per group of equal notes;
- where the human wrote it;
- the human's own words, quoted;
- the answer and what changed, with pushback stated plainly;
- a link to what changed.

Owner's ruling (2026-11-03): instructions only. No engine checks or guardrails to force this: agents do it without them, and v2 stays lean.

**Open for the grill.** Which page patterns the page format must gain (the study lists the gaps), whether one-off review pages are in, and the split of facts between `lavish` and `visualize`.
